---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const roadmap = [
  {
    title: 'Boutique hotels & glamping',
    body: 'The same local curation, installable guest app and on-property upsells, built for multi-room and multi-site operators.',
  },
  {
    title: 'A local partner marketplace',
    body: 'Tour operators, private chefs and rental companies featured in the guides where they’re most relevant.',
  },
  {
    title: 'In-room displays',
    body: 'GuestGuideIQ on the tablets and smart-home screens guests find when they arrive, as well as on their phones.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Why GuestGuideIQ exists: guidebooks shouldn't be a chore to build or a liability to maintain."
>
  <section class="wrap section about-hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">Why we’re building this</span>
    <h1>
      Hosts don’t need another binder. They need a <span class="accent-word">concierge.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The best hosts obsess over the details: the linen, the lighting, the coffee on the counter.
      Then guests open a guidebook that’s half-finished or a year out of date. That isn’t a failing
      of the host. The tools ask too much of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section-tight about-body">
    <p>
      Every digital guidebook we tried had the same flaw. It starts as a blank page and asks you to
      write fifty restaurant descriptions, and the moment you finish it starts going stale. Hours
      change, favorites close, and it’s the guest who finds out, not you.
    </p>
    <p>
      GuestGuideIQ starts with the listing you already have. It builds the guide for you, gives it
      the same care you put into your space, and keeps checking every recommendation so it stays
      true. It should feel like an automated digital concierge, and it should earn its place by
      bringing in revenue through the extras guests are glad to buy.
    </p>
    <p>
      We’re a small, independent team. The founders build the product, and for now they also answer
      every support message themselves. We think staying that close to our hosts is how you build a
      tool they want to keep.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow roadmap-eyebrow">Where we’re headed</span>
      <h2>The operating system for the guest experience.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Short-term rentals come first. After that, we want to bring together great design, local
        knowledge and the smart home everywhere guests stay.
      </p>
      <div class="roadmap">
        {roadmap.map((r) => (
          <div class="roadmap-item">
            <h3>{r.title}</h3>
            <p class="muted">{r.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section-tight about-contact">
    <div class="card">
      <h2>Investors &amp; press</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Want the fuller story or the roadmap, or have a question? Reach out through{' '}
        <a href="/contact/">our contact page</a>. We read every message ourselves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-hero {
    max-width: 820px;
  }

  .about-body {
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    padding-bottom: clamp(48px, 7vw, 80px);
  }

  .roadmap-eyebrow {
    color: var(--marigold);
  }

  .roadmap {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 28px;
    margin-top: 40px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .roadmap {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .roadmap-item {
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(251, 243, 234, 0.2);
    padding-top: 18px;
  }

  .about-contact {
    padding-top: clamp(48px, 7vw, 80px);
  }

  .about-contact .card {
    padding: clamp(26px, 4vw, 36px);
    max-width: 640px;
    background: var(--surface-raised);
  }

  .about-contact h2 {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
</style>
